# What this repository's own compose graph loses when it is cut up by directory. One row: its
# independent loops counted whole, the sum of its loops counted one directory at a time, and
# whether the first survives the second.
#
# Beside `rank/decomposition.sqlc`
#   That relation asks the same question of the graphs the filings make, cut by filing. This asks
#   it of the graph this repository's queries make, cut by directory. The arithmetic is the same and
#   the verdicts differ: the layer graph survives its cut because it has no loops either way, the
#   unit graph does not because its one loop runs through two filings, and this graph does not
#   because most of its loops run through two directories.
#
# A question about finding your way, not an accusation
#   Nothing here is a defect. The compose graph has many loops and they cost nothing, because a
#   query reads a relation the same way however often it is named, so composing a relation twice
#   reads it once. `rank/compose_edges.sqlc` states that, and `rank/cycle_space.sqlc` the opposite
#   verdict for `pm.part`, where the same shape is `checks/jagged_layer`. What the cut measures is
#   whether the directory tree is where the composition lives, and the answer is that it mostly is
#   not.
#
# A template is in one directory; a layer can be under two filings
#   A template lives in exactly one directory, so `sum(n_nodes)` over the scopes equals the whole
#   graph's node count. A layer may be filed under two documents, so the per-filing node counts in
#   `rank/graph_measures.sqlc` sum to more than its graph's. The two relations look alike and their
#   sums do not compare, as `rank/compose_measures.sqlc`'s header explains.
#
# Why the sum can only fall
#   Cutting throws away every edge whose ends are in different directories and keeps every node, so
#   each scope's walk sees part of the whole. An edge that closed a loop across a boundary closes
#   nothing inside one, so the summed loops are at most the whole graph's, and the difference is the
#   number of independent loops that need two directories to exist.
#
# Probe
#   Move a template into the directory of everything it composes and recompose: the summed loops
#   must rise, and `corpus_wide` must not move at all.
-- rank/compose_measures.sqlc at both scopes: the whole against the sum of the parts.
SELECT w.cycle_space_dim                                                   AS corpus_wide,
       coalesce(sum(p.cycle_space_dim), 0)::bigint                         AS summed_per_directory,
       w.cycle_space_dim = coalesce(sum(p.cycle_space_dim), 0)::bigint     AS survives_the_cut,
       count(p.scope)                                                      AS directories,
       w.cycle_space_dim - coalesce(sum(p.cycle_space_dim), 0)::bigint     AS loops_that_cross
FROM      (
    :compose(rank/compose_measures.sqlc)
) w
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(rank/compose_measures.sqlc)
) p ON p.scope IS NOT NULL
WHERE w.scope IS NULL
GROUP BY w.cycle_space_dim
